\begin{abstract}
We investigate neutral $D$-meson mixing and rare charm decays in a Littlest Higgs framework supplemented by an extra charge-$2/3$ weak-singlet quark. The mixing of the heavy vector-like state with the ordinary up-type quarks induces non-unitarity in the effective $3\times 3$ CKM matrix and generates tree-level flavor-changing neutral-current couplings of the $Z$ boson in the up sector. We analyze the resulting contributions to $\Delta C=2$ transitions and to rare $\Delta C=1$ processes, including $D^0$--$\bar D^0$ mixing, $D\to X_u \ell^+\ell^-$, $D\to X_u \nu\bar\nu$, and representative exclusive modes such as $D\to \pi \ell^+\ell^-$. Current constraints from electroweak precision data, CKM unitarity tests, direct searches for vector-like quarks, and flavor observables are imposed in order to delineate the allowed parameter space. We find that the singlet-quark-induced $Z$-mediated flavor-changing couplings can yield contributions to $D^0$--$\bar D^0$ mixing comparable to the present experimental sensitivity, while remaining consistent with existing bounds. Rare charm decays may also receive sizable short-distance enhancements over the Standard Model expectations, although long-distance hadronic effects remain an important limitation for several charged-lepton final states. Modes with neutrinos in the final state provide a cleaner probe of the underlying short-distance dynamics. Our results show that charm flavor observables offer a complementary test of Littlest Higgs scenarios with vector-like up-type quarks and can significantly constrain the flavor structure of the extended quark sector.
\end{abstract}
